WEBVTT

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:03.110
A fossil is preserved evidence of life:

00:00:03.110 --> 00:00:05.210
remains, an imprint or a trace.

00:00:05.470 --> 00:00:09.010
Hard shells, bones and teeth are more likely to survive than

00:00:09.010 --> 00:00:13.110
soft tissue. Rapid burial reduces scavenging, weathering and decay.

00:00:13.390 --> 00:00:14.990
The resulting record is selective;

00:00:14.990 --> 00:00:19.050
the abundance of durable fossils does not mean soft-bodied organisms were

00:00:19.050 --> 00:00:19.450
absent.

00:00:20.130 --> 00:00:23.910
When a buried organism dissolves or decays, its shape can remain

00:00:23.910 --> 00:00:27.510
as an unfilled mold. If minerals later fill that cavity, the

00:00:27.510 --> 00:00:29.990
infill forms a cast. Keep the sequence straight:

00:00:30.550 --> 00:00:34.130
organism, burial, removal, cavity and possible filling.

00:00:34.290 --> 00:00:37.810
A mold and cast preserve related shapes through different material states.

00:00:38.710 --> 00:00:43.030
Minerals can replace original material, as silica replaces wood.

00:00:43.210 --> 00:00:46.870
Compression can leave a carbon film, and quiet sediment can preserve

00:00:46.870 --> 00:00:49.930
an impression. Amber can entomb fragile structures.

00:00:50.430 --> 00:00:53.990
Stromatolites record layered constructions by microscopic organisms.

00:00:54.290 --> 00:00:55.990
These are different preservation routes;

00:00:56.170 --> 00:00:59.210
no single sequence describes every fossil in the chapter.

00:01:00.290 --> 00:01:03.250
Footprints and trackways record movement across a surface.

00:01:03.790 --> 00:01:07.630
Burrows record animals disturbing soft sediment, even when their bodies are

00:01:07.630 --> 00:01:10.850
not preserved. Later sediment can fill these structures.

00:01:11.190 --> 00:01:14.470
Distinguish a body fossil from a trace fossil by asking whether

00:01:14.470 --> 00:01:17.430
you are seeing the organism itself or evidence of what it

00:01:17.430 --> 00:01:17.710
did.

00:01:18.710 --> 00:01:23.310
Branching manganese-oxide dendrites can resemble plants, but they grow through inorganic

00:01:23.310 --> 00:01:27.790
mineral processes. Concretions grow within sediment and can also look biological.

00:01:28.250 --> 00:01:31.230
A concretion may contain a fossil without being a fossil itself.

00:01:31.510 --> 00:01:35.090
Classify the origin of the feature, using its structure and context

00:01:35.090 --> 00:01:37.670
rather than its suggestive outline alone.
